"Put two fingers gently on the front of your throat and say “ahh.” Do you feel a buzz? Now whisper “ahh.” The buzz is gone. That buzz is your voice being made. Your body does it in three steps, and you can remember them with three words: air, buzz, and shape.": "e0cae0c2843c",
"Put two fingers gently on the front of your throat and say “ahh.” Do you feel a buzz? Now whisper “ahh.” The buzz is gone. That buzz is your voice being made. Your body does it in three steps, and you can remember them with three words: air, buzz, and shape. The first step is air. To speak, you breathe out. Your lungs push air up your windpipe, the tube that you met in the lesson about breathing. Without moving air, there is no voice at all. Try to speak while you hold your breath, and you will see. The second step is the buzz. At the top of your windpipe is your voice box, which is also called the larynx. Inside it are two small folds of soft tissue called the vocal folds, or vocal cords. When you breathe, they stay open, and air passes between them without a sound. When you speak, they close. The air pushes them apart, and they snap back together, again and again, chopping the stream of air into tiny puffs. That is the buzz you felt with your fingers. How fast do the vocal folds open and close? Much faster than you can see. In a man's voice, they do it about 125 times every second. In a woman's voice, it is about 210 times, and in a child's voice, more than 300. Faster means a higher sound. To sing a high note, tiny muscles stretch your vocal folds longer and tighter, like a rubber band pulled tight. Children have high voices because their vocal folds are short. As you grow, they get longer, and your voice gets lower. The third step is the shape. A buzz is not a word yet. The sound travels up through your throat and your mouth, and you change it by moving your tongue, your lips, and your jaw. Say “ah,” “ee,” and “oo” on one note. The buzz stays the same, but your mouth makes a different shape for each one, and you hear three different vowels. Some sounds use no buzz at all. Put your fingers on your throat again and say a long “sss,” and then a long “zzz.” Your mouth does exactly the same thing for both. The only difference is that your vocal folds buzz for “zzz” and rest for “sss.” The same is true for “fff” and “vvv.” English is full of pairs like these, and getting the buzz right is a big part of a clear accent. Now try the buzz check below the model.": "9ae77b9e7189",
